*Error Analysis: Off by One Error - IndexError*

*Error Description:*
`IndexError: list index out of range` on line 5 `items[i]`. Code tries to access 4th item when list has only 3 items. `range(len(items)+1)` = range(4) = 0,1,2,3 but valid indexes are only 0,1,2. Last iteration `i=3` crashes.

*Root Cause:*
Line 4 bug: `for i in range(len(items) + 1):`
The `+1` was added to fix display `Item {i+1}` but broke the loop range. Confusion between 1-based human counting and 0-based Python indexing.

*Solution:*
# FIXED CODE
def print_inventory_report(items):
    print("===== INVENTORY REPORT =====")
    for i, item in enumerate(items):
        print(f"Item {i+1}: {item['name']} - Quantity: {item['quantity']}")
    print("============================")

def main():
    items = [
        {"name": "Laptop", "quantity": 15},
        {"name": "Mouse", "quantity": 30},
        {"name": "Keyboard", "quantity": 25}
    ]
    print_inventory_report(items)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
*Learning Points:*
1. Use `enumerate()` instead of `range(len())` to avoid off-by-one
2. `range(3)` already gives 0,1,2 - don't add +1
3. Keep `i+1` for display separate from `items[i]` for access
4. Test edge cases: empty list, 1 item, many items
5. Same bug would crash Task Manager if looping tasks with +1
